treetMap outlines and streets, not photographs. The streets were read off the ground: Fifth Avenue at 29 degrees to north, 104th and 105th Streets 79 m apart; the building was 88 m long and ran 20 m past 105th Street into the roadway, so it is now 61 m along Fifth, between the two side streets' building lines, 48 m deep, round a forecourt on Fifth that the outline shows, six storeys (23.5 m) of red brick over a two-storey limestone base with a belt course, a cornice, corner quoins, a regular window grid on all three street faces, and the museum's glass entrance under its thin canopy on Fifth; a lower block behind, and the seven-storey neighbour further east on the block; Central Park's wall, trees and the Conservatory Garden's paths across the avenue. Still guessed: the depth, the forecourt (its size is read off 7 m cells), the window rhythm, the entrance's exact place, the back block. The ground gives twelve storeys for the block's outline, which does not match the six published; the published height was kept."}